      "starred_url": "https://api.github.com/users/ryanofsky/starred%7B/owner%7D%7B/repo%7D",
      "subscriptions_url": "https://api.github.com/users/ryanofsky/subscriptions",
      "organizations_url": "https://api.github.com/users/ryanofsky/orgs",
      "repos_url": "https://api.github.com/users/ryanofsky/repos",
      "events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/ryanofsky/events%7B/privacy%7D",
      "received_events_url": "https://api.github.com/users/ryanofsky/received_events",
      "type": "User",
      "site_admin": false,
      "name": null,
      "patch_url": null
    },
    "body": "**Problem:** With the `UnstableChain` IPC interface from #29409 merged, an IPC client could make `bitcoin-node` hit an `Assume` failure (debug builds) or compute wrong fees (release builds) by passing a malformed feerate to `broadcastTransaction`, `calculateIndividualBumpFees` or `calculateCombinedBumpFee`. These methods take feerates as serialized `Data` fields, and `CFeeRate` deserialization reads the fee and size without the checks its constructors do, so it accepts a negative size, which fee calculations require to be positive, and a zero size with a nonzero fee, which compares as an infinite feerate. Nothing deserializes a `CFeeRate` on master today.\n\n**Solution:** Make `CFeeRate` deserialization throw on values its constructors cannot produce, the same way other malformed serialized data is rejected. The change is a `SER_READ` check in `src/policy/feerate.h` plus a unit test. It is similar to #36520, which handles other invalid arguments to `Chain` IPC methods.\n\nThis issue was found by Project Loupe and passed on by instagibbs.",
